{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":27266,"duration_api_ms":28395,"num_turns":10,"result":"I wrote the script to `src/49-cpufreq/cpufreq.bt`, but I haven't run it. The sandbox blocked my check for whether bpftrace is installed, so it's untested.\n\nIt traces the `power:cpu_frequency` tracepoint and does four things:\n- **Live log:** prints each frequency change as a line with time, CPU, old MHz and new MHz.\n- **Residency:** keeps `@residency_ms[cpu, MHz]`, the milliseconds each CPU has spent at each frequency. It prints this every 10 seconds.\n- **Counts and histogram:** keeps `@transitions[cpu]` and a histogram `@freq_mhz` in 200 MHz buckets.\n- **Exit:** clears the scratch maps in `END` so only the results print when you stop it.\n\nRun it with `sudo bpftrace src/49-cpufreq/cpufreq.bt`.\n\nSome drivers change frequency in hardware and never fire this tracepoint. `intel_pstate` in active mode and `amd-pstate-epp` are examples. If you see no output, check the driver with `cpupower frequency-info`.\n\nI didn't add a README or Makefile. Say if you want them to match the other tutorials.","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"18eb03b4-f2ef-40cb-839e-4a551194df59","total_cost_usd":0.11092854999999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":16,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3533,"cache_read_input_tokens":183636,"output_tokens":2806,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3533},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":408,"cache_read_input_tokens":28144,"cache_creation_input_tokens":670,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":670,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":351,"outputTokens":20,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.000451,"con